Recall the self-map digraph $\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}}$, with vertices the self-maps of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}$, and an edge $(f,g) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}})$ between self-maps $f$ and $g$ if and only if for every edge $(x,y) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}})$, we have that $(f(x),g(y)) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}})$. In the self-map digraph, the endomorphisms are precisely the looped vertices. Consider the homomorphism $\varphi: \ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n ightarrow \ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}}$ induced by an $n+1$-ary polymorphism of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$ where $n \geq 1$; suppose for a contradiction that the polymorphism depends on all its variables. Then clearly the image of $\varphi$ contains at least two elements, and at least one of these elements is not a constant map. 
oindent{ f Claim 0.} {\em The only loops in $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}}$ are the constant maps 1 and 2 and the identity. } 
oindent {\em Proof of Claim 0}: let $f$ be an endomorphism of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}$. Then $f$ maps loops to loops. Since $(1,2)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$, $f$ can either map both to 1, both to 2 , or fix both. Clearly if it fixes both $f$ is the identity. Otherwise, if $f(1)=f(2)=i$, then $(f(0),i) , (i,f(0)) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ so $f(0)=i$. Without fear of confusion, we denote the constant maps by 0, 1 and 2. We denote the identity map by $id$. 
oindent{ f Claim 1.} {\em (a) There are no edges in $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}}$ between the identity and the constant maps; (b) if $(2,f), (f,1) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$, then $f=0$; (c) if $c$ is a constant map such that $(f,c),(c,f) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ then $f = c$. } 
oindent {\em Proof of Claim 1}: $(2,f) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ implies $(2,f(i)) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ for all $i$ so $f(i) \in \{0,2\}$ for all $i$. Similarly $(f,1)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ implies $f(i) \in \{0,1\}$ for all $i$ so (b) follows immediately. To prove (a), observe that any in- or out-neighbour of a constant cannot be surjective since no constant has in- or out-neighbourhood of size 3. For (c), argue again as we just did: $(1,f) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ means $f(i) \in \{1,2\}$ for all $i$, and $(f,1)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ implies $f(i) \in \{0,1\}$ for all $i$ and hence $f=1$; the proof for $c=2$ is identical. 
oindent{ f Claim 2.} {\em If $(f,id),(id,f) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ then $f = id$. } 
oindent {\em Proof of Claim 2}: Assume that $(f,id),(id,f) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$. If $i$ is a loop then $(i,i)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ implies $(f(i),i),(i,f(i))\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ thus $f(i) = i$. Now $(0,1)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ implies $(f(0),1)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$, and $(2,0)\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ implies $(2,f(0))\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}})$ so $f(0)=0$. Obviously the loops of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n$ are precisely the tuples whose coordinates belong to $\{1,2\}$. Call the subdigraph induced by these vertices $\ensuremath{\mathrm{W}}$, and notice it is weakly connected. Then the homomorphism $\varphi$ must map $\ensuremath{\mathrm{W}}$ onto a weakly connected digraph consisting of loops only. By Claim 1 (a) either (i) $\ensuremath{\mathrm{W}}$ is entirely mapped to $\{id\}$ or (ii) $\ensuremath{\mathrm{W}}$ is mapped by $\varphi$ to the set $\{1,2\}$. Choose any tuple $X$ of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n$ containing a coordinate equal to 0. Then there exist $Y, Z \in W$ such that $(X,Z), (Z,Y), (Y,X) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n)$: indeed, let $Z$ be obtained from $X$ by replacing each 0 entry by 1, fixing all other coordinates, and let $Y$ be obtained from $X$ by replacing each 0 entry by 2, fixing all other coordinates. Thus in case (i) we get that $id = \varphi(Y), (\varphi(Y),\varphi(X)),(\varphi(X),\varphi(Z)) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ and $\varphi(Z)=id$ so by Claim 2 $\varphi$ maps all of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n$ to $id$. In case (ii), we get that $\varphi(Y)$ and $\varphi(Z)$ belong to $\{1,2\}$; since $(\varphi(Z),\varphi(Y))\in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ we must in fact have that $(\varphi(Z),\varphi(Y))$ belongs to $\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,2)\}$; thus we get one of the following cases, namely $(1,\varphi(X)),(\varphi(X),1) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ or $(1,\varphi(X)),(\varphi(X),2) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$ or $(2,\varphi(X)),(\varphi(X),2) \in E(\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^{\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}})$. By Claim 1 (b) and (c) $\varphi(X)$ must be one of the constant maps 0, 1 or 2. Thus we conclude that $\varphi$ either maps all of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n$ to $\{id\}$, in which case our polymorphism cannot depend on its first $n$ variables; or otherwise $\varphi$ maps all of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}}^n$ to constant maps, and then the polymorphism does not depend on its last variable, also a contradiction.
